From 2c77adec7f2a8262940157a8f00789718fa28966 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 03:26:58 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: what the display is sent is read from the
 driver, and its transfer function is a label of its own

Player.Process(videooutputhdrtype) said what Kodi had asked for (the EOTF of
SetHDR()), not what the driver holds: a commit that the driver refused, or
metadata that another request carried, made the label differ from the signal. The
overview page of the player information already read the content of the blob of
HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA, but only with the page open.

The blob is now read once for each id it has (CHDROutputRead, a new header with no
DRM in it so that a test runs on the host), where CHDRSignal::Settle() already asks
the kernel for the id and, from the id as the kernel took it with the last accepted
commit, when the labels are published (no call to the kernel). Both labels come from
that reading:

- Player.Process(videooutputhdrtype): dolbyvision, hdr10plus, hdr10 (PQ), hlg, sdr;
  empty where the driver does not say (no HDR_OUTPUT_METADATA on the connector, a
  blob that cannot be read, or traditional gamma with an HDR range, which has no
  name among these). Before, a connector without the property said sdr.
- Player.Process(videooutputeotf), new: sdr, hdrgamma, pq, hlg; empty where it is
  not known. A PQ output is not called an HDR10 video, as vertox asked. No blob is
  no HDR InfoFrame, which a display takes as SDR.

Both are documented in GUIInfoManager.cpp. Published as properties of the Home
window (hades.out.hdrtype, hades.out.eotf), as the other labels of the output.
